## Question J5 6.1
$mc3 = new_checkbox_multiple_choice();

$mc3 -> qa (
"Where does the proof use that \( U \) is path-independent?", 
"Line 3","Line 6"
);

$mc3->makeLast("Line 1","Line 2","Line 3","Line 4","Line 5","Line 6","Line 7","Line 8","Line 9"
);


###########################
#  Main text

Context()->texStrings;
BEGIN_TEXT
Theorem I. Let \(F\) be a vector field in \( \mathbb{R}^n \) continuous on an open path-connected set \( U \subseteq \mathbb{R}^n \). $BR
Then the following are equivalent:  $BR
(a) There exists a \( C^1 \) function \( f : U \to \mathbb{R} \) such that \( F = \nabla f \) on \( U \). $BR
(b) For any two oriented piecewise curves \( C_1, C_2 \) in \( U \) with the same start and end points,
\[  \int_{C_1} F \cdot d \gamma = \int_{C_2} F \cdot d \gamma \]
(c) For any closed piecewise curve \( C \) in \( U \),
\[  \int_{C} F \cdot d \gamma = 0\]
Refer to any such vector field \( F \) as conservative on \( U\). $BR $BR

Below is a proof that Theorem I(b) implies Theorem I(a). In other words, path-independent vector fields are gradient vector fields. As usual, important details are missing.
$BR
\{ image( "j5-q6-proof.png",width=>791, height=>526) \}
$BR
1. \{ $mc1 -> print_q() \}
\{ $mc1 -> print_a() \}
$BR


2.  \{ $mc2 -> print_q() \}
\{ $mc2 -> print_a() \}
$BR


3. \{ $mc3 -> print_q() \}
\{ $mc3 -> print_a() \}

END_TEXT




